e":"Variable-size sliding window over a string","lc":3},{"id":"longest-repeating-replacement","title":"Longest Repeating Character Replacement","subtitle":"Variable window · invariant len − maxFreq ≤ k","lc":424},{"id":"min-window-substring","title":"Minimum Window Substring","subtitle":"Smallest window of s covering all of t","lc":76},{"id":"permutation-in-string","title":"Permutation in String","subtitle":"Fixed-size window + frequency match","lc":567},{"id":"sliding-window-maximum","title":"Sliding Window Maximum","subtitle":"Monotonic deque · the front is always the window max","lc":239}]},{"id":"stack","title":"Stack","problems":[{"id":"intro","title":"Overview","subtitle":"LIFO · push, pop, and why \"the top\" matters"},{"id":"valid-parentheses","title":"Valid Parentheses","subtitle":"The top is the bracket that must close next","lc":20},{"id":"baseball-game","title":"Baseball Game","subtitle":"Every operation is about the top","lc":682},{"id":"decode-string","title":"Decode String","subtitle":"k[body] with nesting · \"]\" collapses the top","lc":394},{"id":"longest-valid-parentheses","title":"Longest Valid Parentheses","subtitle":"Stack of indices · a base below every run","lc":32},{"id":"monotonic-intro","title":"Monotonic Stack","subtitle":"Keep it sorted · bigger arrivals resolve the waiters"},{"id":"daily-temperatures","title":"Daily Temperatures","subtitle":"Monotonic stack · new days resolve waiting days","lc":739},{"id":"largest-rectangle-histogram","title":"Largest Rectangle in Histogram","subtitle":"Increasing stack · shorter bars close rectangles","lc":84},{"id":"min-stack","title":"Min Stack","subtitle":"O(1) getMin · a twin min-stack","lc":155},{"id":"evaluate-rpn","title":"Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation","subtitle":"Operand stack · postfix evaluation","lc":150}]},{"id":"linked-list","title":"Linked List","problems":[{"id":"intro","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Nodes, next-pointers, and O(1) surgery"},{"id":"reverse-linked-list","title":"Reverse Linked List","subtitle":"Flip every arrow · prev / curr / next","lc":206},{"id":"merge-two-sorted-lists","title":"Merge Two Sorted Lists","subtitle":"Splice the smaller head · dummy node","lc":21},{"id":"add-two-numbers","title":"Add Two Numbers","subtitle":"Elementary addition · carry","lc":2},{"id":"linked-list-cycle","title":"Linked List Cycle","subtitle":"Floyd's tortoise & hare","lc":141},{"id":"palindrome-linked-list","title":"Palindrome Linked List","subtitle":"Middle + reverse half + two pointers","lc":234},{"id":"remove-nth-from-end","title":"Remove Nth Node From End","subtitle":"Two pointers locked n apart","lc":19},{"id":"reorder-list","title":"Reorder List","subtitle":"Middle + reverse + alternate merge","lc":143},{"id":"swap-nodes-in-pairs","title":"Swap Nodes in Pairs","subtitle":"Three arrows per pair · dummy head","lc":24},{"id":"lru-cache","title":"LRU Cache","subtitle":"Hash map + doubly linked list · O(1) get / put","lc":146}]},{"id":"heap","title":"Heap","problems":[{"id":"intro","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Complete tree + heap property · sift-up / sift-down"},{"id":"kth-largest-element","title":"Kth Largest Element in an Array","subtitle":"Min-heap of size k as a top-k gate","lc":215},{"id":"k-closest-points","title":"K Closest Points to Origin","subtitle":"Max-heap of size k, keyed by distance","lc":973},{"id":"find-k-closest-elements","title":"Find K Closest Elements","subtitle":"Max-heap keyed by (distance, value)","lc":658},{"id":"merge-k-sorted-lists","title":"Merge K Sorted Lists","subtitle":"Min-heap of the k current heads","lc":23},{"id":"median-from-data-stream","title":"Median from Data Stream","subtitle":"Two heaps straddling the middle","lc":295},{"id":"task-scheduler","title":"Task Scheduler","subtitle":"Most-frequent-first · max-heap vs. the frame formula","lc":621},{"id":"relative-ranks","title":"Relative Ranks","subtitle":"Sort the indices so the scores keep their owners","lc":506}]},{"id":"binary-search","title":"Binary Search","problems":[{"id":"intro","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Halve the search space · then search answers, not arrays"},{"id":"binary-search","title":"Binary Search","subtitle":"The canonical template · halve [lo, hi]","lc":704},{"id":"search-insert-position","title":"Search Insert Position","subtitle":"Where lo lands when the target is missing","lc":35},{"id":"peak-index-mountain","title":"Peak Index in a Mountain Array","subtitle":"Binary search without a sorted array","lc":852},{"id":"max-candies-k-children","title":"Maximum Candies Allocated to K Children","subtitle":"Binary search the answer, not the array","lc":2226},{"id":"koko-eating-bananas","title":"Apple Harvest (Koko Eating Bananas)","subtitle":"Binary search on the answer · feasibility probe","lc":875},{"id":"search-rotated-sorted","title":"Search in Rotated Sorted Array","subtitle":"One half is always sorted, use it to steer","lc":33},{"id":"find-min-rotated","title":"Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array","subtitle":"Compare mid to hi to find the cliff","lc":153},{"id":"search-2d-matrix","title":"Search a 2D Matrix","subtitle":"Treat the grid as one flat sorted array · binary search 0..m·n−1","lc":74},{"id":"split-array-largest-sum","title":"Split Array Largest Sum","subtitle":"Binary search the cap · greedy feasibility","lc":410},{"id":"kth-smallest-sorted-matrix","title":"Kth Smallest in a Sorted Matrix","subtitle":"Binary search on values · count ≤ x probes","lc":378},{"id":"minimum-shipping-capacity","title":"Minimum Shipping Capacity","subtitle":"Ship packages in D days · search the capacity","lc":1011}]},{"id":"dfs","title":"Depth-First Search","problems":[{"id":"introduction","title":"Introduction","subtitle":"Go deep, then backtrack · pre/in/post order"},{"id":"fundamentals","title":"Fundamentals","subtitle":"The recursion template: base case → recurse → combine"},{"id":"return-values","title":"Return Values","subtitle":"Bubbling answers UP with post-order"},{"id":"maximum-depth","title":"Maximum Depth of Binary Tree","subtitle":"Depth = 1 + max(left, right)","lc":104},{"id":"path-sum","title":"Path Sum","subtitle":"Root-to-leaf sum == target · carry the remainder down","lc":112},{"id":"passing-values","title":"Passing Values Down and Up","subtitle":"Parameters go down, returns come up"},{"id":"validate-bst","title":"Validate Binary Search Tree","subtitle":"Carry (low, high) bounds down","lc":98},{"id":"calculate-tilt","title":"Binary Tree Tilt","subtitle":"Post-order: return subtree sums, accumulate |L − R|","lc":563},{"id":"diameter","title":"Diameter of a Binary Tree","subtitle":"Post-order heights, track the widest split","lc":543},{"id":"path-sum-ii","title":"Path Sum II","subtitle":"Backtracking: collect every root-to-leaf path == target","lc":113},{"id":"longest-univalue-path","title":"Longest Univalue Path","subtitle":"Longest chain of equal values","lc":687},{"id":"invert-tree","title":"Invert Binary Tree","subtitle":"Pre-order DFS, swap every node's children","lc":226},{"id":"same-tree","title":"Same Tree","subtitle":"Lockstep pre-order DFS over both trees","lc":100},{"id":"lowest-common-ancestor","title":"Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Tree","subtitle":"Post-order DFS, bubble a target up, split point wins","lc":236},{"id":"binary-tree-max-path-sum","title":"Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum","subtitle":"Post-order gains · clamp negative arms","lc":124},{"id":"serialize-deserialize","title":"Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree","subtitle":"Pre-order with null markers, then rebuild from the stream","lc":297},{"id":"graphs-overview","title":"Graphs Overview","subtitle":"Nodes, edges, and why DFS needs a visited set"},{"id":"adjacency-list","title":"Adjacency List","subtitle":"How to store a graph"},{"id":"copy-graph","title":"Copy Graph","subtitle":"Deep clone with an old→new map","lc":133},{"id":"graph-valid-tree","title":"Graph Valid Tree","subtitle":"Connected + exactly n−1 edges + no cycle","lc":261},{"id":"grid-dfs-overview","title":"Matrices","subtitle":"A grid is a graph · DFS the 4 neighbours"},{"id":"flood-fill","title":"Flood Fill","subtitle":"Recolour a connected region","lc":733},{"id":"number-of-islands","title":"Number of Islands","subtitle":"Count components, sink each island","lc":200},{"id":"surrounded-regions","title":"Surrounded Regions","subtitle":"Border-connected O's are safe","lc":130},{"id":"pacific-atlantic","title":"Pacific Atlantic Water Flow","subtitle":"Flood inward from both oceans, intersect","lc":417}]},{"id":"greedy","title":"Greedy Algorithms","problems":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Take the best local choice, never look back"},{"id":"best-time-buy-sell-stock","title":"Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock","subtitle":"Track the cheapest day seen so far","lc":121},{"id":"gas-station","title":"Gas Station","subtitle":"If total gas ≥ total cost, one start works, find it","lc":134},{"id":"jump-game","title":"Jump Game","subtitle":"Track the farthest index you can reach","lc":55},{"id":"jump-game-ii","title":"Jump Game II","subtitle":"Minimum jumps, greedy BFS by levels","lc":45},{"id":"partition-labels","title":"Partition Labels","subtitle":"Extend the part to each letter’s last occurrence","lc":763}]},{"id":"dp","title":"Dynamic Programming","problems":[{"id":"fundamentals","title":"Fundamentals","subtitle":"Overlapping subproblems · a table you fill once"},{"id":"solving-with-dp","title":"Solving a Question with Dynamic Programming","subtitle":"State → recurrence → base case → order → answer"},{"id":"pascals-triangle","title":"Pascal's Triangle","subtitle":"The smallest recurrence there is","lc":118},{"id":"climbing-stairs","title":"Climbing Stairs","subtitle":"ways(i) = ways(i−1) + ways(i−2) · Fibonacci","lc":70},{"id":"dice-combinations","title":"Dice Combinations","subtitle":"dp[i] = Σ dp[i−j] for j in 1..6 · a 6-wide window","lc":377},{"id":"max-subarray","title":"Maximum Subarray","subtitle":"cur = max(nums[i], cur + nums[i]) · Kadane's","lc":53},{"id":"house-robber","title":"House Robber","subtitle":"dp[i] = max(dp[i−1], dp[i−2] + nums[i])","lc":198},{"id":"coin-change","title":"Coin Change","subtitle":"dp[a] = 1 + min(dp[a − coin]) · fewest coins to make amount","lc":322},{"id":"longest-common-subsequence","title":"Longest Common Subsequence","subtitle":"Grid DP · match → diagonal+1, else max(up, left)","lc":1143},{"id":"edit-distance","title":"Edit Distance","subtitle":"Grid DP · dp[i][j] = min edits word1[:i] → word2[:j]","lc":72},{"id":"counting-bits","title":"Counting Bits","subtitle":"dp[i] = dp[i>>1] + (i & 1)","lc":338},{"id":"decode-ways","title":"Decode Ways","subtitle":"1 or 2 digits at a time","lc":91},{"id":"unique-paths","title":"Unique Paths","subtitle":"Grid DP · dp[i][j] = dp[i−1][j] + dp[i][j−1]","lc":62},{"id":"maximal-square","title":"Maximal Square","subtitle":"Grid DP · dp[i][j] = min(up, left, diag) + 1","lc":221},{"id":"longest-increasing-subsequence","title":"Longest Increasing Subsequence","subtitle":"dp[i] = longest increasing run ENDING at i","lc":300},{"id":"word-break","title":"Word Break","subtitle":"dp[i] = can s[0..i) be split into dictionary words","lc":139},{"id":"maximum-profit-job-scheduling","title":"Maximum Profit in Job Scheduling","subtitle":"Sort by end; take or skip","lc":1235},{"id":"paint-house","title":"Paint House","subtitle":"Grid DP · dp[i][c] = cost + min(other two colours)","lc":256},{"id":"paint-house-ii","title":"Paint House II","subtitle":"k colours · track the two smallest → O(n·k)","lc":265},{"id":"minimum-window-subsequence","title":"Minimum Window Subsequence","subtitle":"Smallest window of S containing T as a subsequence","lc":727}]},{"id":"graphs","title":"Graphs","problems":[{"id":"graphs-overview","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Directed, weighted, and how we store them"},{"id":"course-schedule","title":"Course Schedule","subtitle":"Can you finish? = is it a DAG (no cycle)?","lc":207},{"id":"course-schedule-ii","title":"Course Schedule II","subtitle":"Return a valid topological order","lc":210},{"id":"shortest-path-algorithms","title":"Shortest Path Algorithms","subtitle":"BFS vs Dijkstra vs Bellman-Ford"},{"id":"network-delay-time","title":"Network Delay Time","subtitle":"Dijkstra · finalize the nearest unfinished node","lc":743},{"id":"cheapest-flights","title":"Cheapest Flights Within K Stops","subtitle":"Bellman-Ford bounded to K+1 hops","lc":787},{"id":"path-minimum-effort","title":"Path With Minimum Effort","subtitle":"Dijkstra on a grid · minimize the max step","lc":1631},{"id":"find-the-city","title":"Find the City With Fewest Reachable","subtitle":"Floyd-Warshall all-pairs, then count","lc":1334},{"id":"union-find","title":"Union-Find (DSU)","subtitle":"Disjoint sets · find + union"},{"id":"connected-components","title":"Number of Connected Components","subtitle":"Union-Find · count = n, merge drops count","lc":323},{"id":"redundant-connection","title":"Redundant Connection","subtitle":"Union-Find · first cycle-closing edge","lc":684},{"id":"word-ladder","title":"Word Ladder","subtitle":"Shortest transformation sequence via BFS","lc":127}]},{"id":"backtracking","title":"Backtracking","problems":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Choose → explore → un-choose"},{"id":"word-search","title":"Word Search","subtitle":"DFS with mark-and-unmark backtracking","lc":79},{"id":"solution-space-trees","title":"Solution Space Trees","subtitle":"Nodes = partial solutions, edges = choices"},{"id":"subsets","title":"Subsets","subtitle":"Every node of the tree is a subset","lc":78},{"id":"permutations","title":"Permutations","subtitle":"Choose an unused element at each level","lc":46},{"id":"letter-combinations","title":"Letter Combinations of a Phone Number","subtitle":"One digit per tree level","lc":17},{"id":"generate-parentheses","title":"Generate Parentheses","subtitle":"Add '(' or ')' with validity pruning","lc":22},{"id":"combination-sum","title":"Combination Sum","subtitle":"Reuse allowed; prune when the sum overshoots","lc":39},{"id":"palindrome-partitioning","title":"Palindrome Partitioning","subtitle":"Cut only where the prefix is a palindrome","lc":131},{"id":"n-queens","title":"N-Queens","subtitle":"Place row by row, backtrack on attack","lc":51}]},{"id":"bfs","title":"Breadth-First Search","problems":[{"id":"introduction","title":"Introduction","subtitle":"Level by level with a FIFO queue"},{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Process one whole level per iteration"},{"id":"level-order-sum","title":"Level Order Sum","subtitle":"Sum each level with the level-batching loop","lc":1161},{"id":"rightmost-node","title":"Rightmost Node","subtitle":"Right-side view = last node of each level","lc":199},{"id":"zigzag-level-order","title":"Zigzag Level Order","subtitle":"Alternate L→R, R→L each level","lc":103},{"id":"maximum-width","title":"Maximum Width of Binary Tree","subtitle":"Index nodes by position 2i, 2i+1","lc":662},{"id":"graphs-overview","title":"Graphs Overview","subtitle":"BFS finds the shortest path (fewest edges)"},{"id":"minimum-knight-moves","title":"Minimum Knight Moves","subtitle":"BFS = shortest path on the move-graph","lc":1197},{"id":"rotting-oranges","title":"Rotting Oranges","subtitle":"Multi-source BFS · rot spreads one ring per minute","lc":994},{"id":"01-matrix","title":"01 Matrix","subtitle":"Distance to nearest 0 · multi-source BFS","lc":542},{"id":"bus-routes","title":"Bus Routes","subtitle":"BFS over routes: each level = one more bus","lc":815}]},{"id":"trie","title":"Trie","problems":[{"id":"intro","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Prefix tree · shared prefixes stored once"},{"id":"implement-trie","title":"Implement Trie Methods","subtitle":"insert · search · startsWith","lc":208},{"id":"prefix-matching","title":"Prefix Matching","subtitle":"Autocomplete · walk prefix, collect subtree","lc":1268}]},{"id":"prefix-sum","title":"Prefix Sum","problems":[{"id":"intro","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Precompute running totals · range sum in O(1)"},{"id":"count-vowels-substrings","title":"Count Vowels in Substrings","subtitle":"Prefix-sum a 0/1 indicator"},{"id":"subarray-sum-equals-k","title":"Subarray Sum Equals K","subtitle":"Running prefix + a count hashmap","lc":560}]},{"id":"matrices","title":"Matrices","problems":[{"id":"spiral-matrix","title":"Spiral Matrix","subtitle":"Four shrinking boundaries","lc":54},{"id":"rotate-image","title":"Rotate Image","subtitle":"90° in place · transpose+reverse or 4-cycles","lc":48},{"id":"set-matrix-zeroes","title":"Set Matrix Zeroes","subtitle":"Find then zero · markers in the first row/col","lc":73},{"id":"island-perimeter","title":"Island Perimeter","subtitle":"Count contributions, not the outline","lc":463},{"id":"missing-and-repeated","title":"Find Missing and Repeated Values","subtitle":"Two equations, two unknowns","lc":2965}]},{"id":"intervals","title":"Intervals","problems":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Sort, then sweep · overlap ⇔ b.start ≤ a.end"},{"id":"merge-intervals","title":"Merge Intervals","subtitle":"Sort by start · extend while overlapping","lc":56},{"id":"insert-interval","title":"Insert Interval","subtitle":"Sorted list · three-phase sweep · no sort","lc":57},{"id":"non-overlapping-intervals","title":"Non-overlapping Intervals","subtitle":"Sort by end · greedily keep early finishers","lc":435},{"id":"meeting-rooms","title":"Meeting Rooms","subtitle":"Sort by start · check neighbours overlap","lc":252},{"id":"meeting-rooms-ii","title":"Meeting Rooms II","subtitle":"Chronological sweep · peak overlap = rooms","lc":253}]},{"id":"bit-manipulation","title":"Bit Manipulation","problems":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","subtitle":"AND · OR · XOR · shifts · the classic tricks"},{"id":"single-number","title":"Single Number","subtitle":"XOR fold · pairs cancel","lc":136},{"id":"number-of-1-bits","title":"Number of 1 Bits","subtitle":"Hamming weight · n & (n − 1)","lc":191},{"id":"missing-number","title":"Missing Number","subtitle":"XOR index ⊕ value · pairs cancel","lc":268},{"id":"reverse-bits","title":"Reverse Bits","subtitle":"build the answer bit by bit","lc":190},{"id":"sum-of-two-integers","title":"Sum of Two Integers","subtitle":"XOR sum + carry · add without +","lc":371}]}],"activePattern":"bfs","activeProblem":"graphs-overview"}],"$L19"]}]
1a:I[28440,["2619","static/chunks/2619-04bc32f026a0d946.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6489","static/chunks/6489-699922ea493f9867.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","1356","static/chunks/1356-9e2757ba007ad1f9.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","951","static/chunks/951-3dbda3a86d4c6444.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","2799","static/chunks/2799-ce430b82b11995d5.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6589","static/chunks/6589-cfdc115b042e146b.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","3650","static/chunks/3650-106e80ed926f59bf.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","8440","static/chunks/8440-bfb72547be677d52.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","134","static/chunks/app/%5Bpattern%5D/%5BproblemId%5D/page-f9e02a54f5641ad9.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs"],"AnimationRunner"]
19:["$","$L1a","graphs-overview",{"problem":{"id":"graphs-overview","patternId":"bfs","title":"Graphs Overview","subtitle":"BFS finds the shortest path (fewest edges)","kind":"concept","input":[],"render":"graph","approaches":[{"label":"BFS shortest paths","complexity":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V)"},"pseudocode":["bfs(graph, source):","    dist = {}","    dist[source] = 0","    queue = [source]","    while queue not empty:","        u = queue.dequeue()      // front","        for v in adj[u]:","            if v not in dist:","                dist[v] = dist[u] + 1","                queue.enqueue(v)  // back","    // dist[v] = fewest edges from source"],"steps":[{"codeLine":1,"narration":"Breadth-First Search explores a graph in RINGS around the source. Ring 0 is the source itself; ring 1 is everything one edge away; ring 2 is two edges away; and so on. The big payoff: BFS finds the SHORTEST PATH (fewest edges) from the source to every node. Here A reaches the far node F two ways: A→B→D→F and A→C→E→F, and BFS will show why the FIRST time we touch a node is always along a shortest route.","graph":{"nodes":[{"id":"A","label":"A","x":2,"y":0},{"id":"B","label":"B","x":0.5,"y":1.5},{"id":"C","label":"C","x":3.5,"y":1.5},{"id":"D","label":"D","x":1,"y":3.2},{"id":"E","label":"E","x":3.2,"y":3.2},{"id":"F","label":"F","x":2.1,"y":4.6}],"edges":[{"from":"A","to":"B"},{"from":"A","to":"C"},{"from":"B","to":"D"},{"from":"C","to":"E"},{"from":"C","to":"D"},{"from":"D","to":"F"},{"from":"E","to":"F"}],"activeId":"$undefined","visitedIds":[],"frontierIds":[],"notes":{},"queue":[],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A"},"vars":[["nodes (V)",6],["edges (E)",7],["source","A"]]},{"codeLine":2,"narration":"The engine is a QUEUE (first-in, first-out). We also track dist[node] = number of edges from the source. We start by giving the source distance 0 and dropping it into the queue. Every other node has distance \"unknown\" for now.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"$undefined","visitedIds":[],"frontierIds":[],"notes":{},"queue":[],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["A"]},"vars":[["queue","[]"],["dist","all unknown"]]},{"codeLine":3,"narration":"Seed: dist[A] = 0 and enqueue A. A is now discovered (blue), it sits on the frontier waiting to be expanded. Watch the distances fill in level by level as the rings grow outward.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"$undefined","visitedIds":[],"frontierIds":["A"],"notes":{"A":"d=0"},"queue":[{"label":"A","sub":"d=0"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["A"]},"vars":[["dist[A]",0],["queue","[A]"]]},{"codeLine":5,"narration":"DEQUEUE the front: node A. We pop it from the front of the queue and VISIT it (paint it green). Its distance dist[A] = 0 is now final, it can never be improved, because BFS reached it on the earliest possible ring.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"A","visitedIds":["A"],"frontierIds":[],"notes":{"A":"d=0"},"queue":[],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A"},"vars":[["dequeued","A"],["dist","A=0"],["queue size",0]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at A's neighbour B. B has no distance yet → it's brand new. We reach it from A, so dist[B] = dist[A] + 1 = 1. This is necessarily a shortest path: A sat on the smallest possible ring, and B is exactly one edge further out.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"A","visitedIds":["A"],"frontierIds":[],"notes":{"A":"d=0"},"queue":[],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["B"],"edgeHighlights":[["A","B"]]},"vars":[["from","A"],["neighbour","B"],["known?","no → set"]]},{"codeLine":8,"narration":"Set dist[B] = 1 and ENQUEUE B at the BACK (it turns blue, discovered, awaiting its turn). Because the queue is FIFO, every node already waiting (all on ring 0 or earlier) gets visited before B. That ordering is exactly what keeps BFS expanding ring by ring.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"A","visitedIds":["A"],"frontierIds":["B"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1"},"queue":[{"label":"B","sub":"d=1"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["B"],"edgeHighlights":[["A","B"]]},"vars":[["set","dist[B]=1"],["enqueue","B"],["queue size",1]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at A's neighbour C. C has no distance yet → it's brand new. We reach it from A, so dist[C] = dist[A] + 1 = 1. This is necessarily a shortest path: A sat on the smallest possible ring, and C is exactly one edge further out.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"A","visitedIds":["A"],"frontierIds":["B"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1"},"queue":[{"label":"B","sub":"d=1"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["C"],"edgeHighlights":[["A","C"]]},"vars":[["from","A"],["neighbour","C"],["known?","no → set"]]},{"codeLine":8,"narration":"Set dist[C] = 1 and ENQUEUE C at the BACK (it turns blue, discovered, awaiting its turn). Because the queue is FIFO, every node already waiting (all on ring 0 or earlier) gets visited before C. That ordering is exactly what keeps BFS expanding ring by ring.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"A","visitedIds":["A"],"frontierIds":["B","C"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1"},"queue":[{"label":"B","sub":"d=1"},{"label":"C","sub":"d=1"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["C"],"edgeHighlights":[["A","C"]]},"vars":[["set","dist[C]=1"],["enqueue","C"],["queue size",2]]},{"codeLine":5,"narration":"DEQUEUE the front: node B. We pop it from the front of the queue and VISIT it (paint it green). Its distance dist[B] = 1 is now final, it can never be improved, because BFS reached it on the earliest possible ring.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"B","visitedIds":["A","B"],"frontierIds":["C"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1"},"queue":[{"label":"C","sub":"d=1"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A"},"vars":[["dequeued","B"],["dist","B=1"],["queue size",1]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at B's neighbour A. A already has a distance (dist[A] = 0), it was discovered on an equal or earlier ring, so we DON'T overwrite it. The first distance assigned is the shortest, and we never touch it again.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"B","visitedIds":["A","B"],"frontierIds":["C"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1"},"queue":[{"label":"C","sub":"d=1"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["A"],"edgeHighlights":[["B","A"]]},"vars":[["from","B"],["neighbour","A"],["known?","yes (d=0)"]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at B's neighbour D. D has no distance yet → it's brand new. We reach it from B, so dist[D] = dist[B] + 1 = 2. This is necessarily a shortest path: B sat on the smallest possible ring, and D is exactly one edge further out.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"B","visitedIds":["A","B"],"frontierIds":["C"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1"},"queue":[{"label":"C","sub":"d=1"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["D"],"edgeHighlights":[["B","D"]]},"vars":[["from","B"],["neighbour","D"],["known?","no → set"]]},{"codeLine":8,"narration":"Set dist[D] = 2 and ENQUEUE D at the BACK (it turns blue, discovered, awaiting its turn). Because the queue is FIFO, every node already waiting (all on ring 1 or earlier) gets visited before D. That ordering is exactly what keeps BFS expanding ring by ring.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"B","visitedIds":["A","B"],"frontierIds":["C","D"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2"},"queue":[{"label":"C","sub":"d=1"},{"label":"D","sub":"d=2"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["D"],"edgeHighlights":[["B","D"]]},"vars":[["set","dist[D]=2"],["enqueue","D"],["queue size",2]]},{"codeLine":5,"narration":"DEQUEUE the front: node C. We pop it from the front of the queue and VISIT it (paint it green). Its distance dist[C] = 1 is now final, it can never be improved, because BFS reached it on the earliest possible ring.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"C","visitedIds":["A","B","C"],"frontierIds":["D"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2"},"queue":[{"label":"D","sub":"d=2"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A"},"vars":[["dequeued","C"],["dist","C=1"],["queue size",1]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at C's neighbour A. A already has a distance (dist[A] = 0), it was discovered on an equal or earlier ring, so we DON'T overwrite it. The first distance assigned is the shortest, and we never touch it again.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"C","visitedIds":["A","B","C"],"frontierIds":["D"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2"},"queue":[{"label":"D","sub":"d=2"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["A"],"edgeHighlights":[["C","A"]]},"vars":[["from","C"],["neighbour","A"],["known?","yes (d=0)"]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at C's neighbour D. D already has a distance (dist[D] = 2), it was discovered on an equal or earlier ring, so we DON'T overwrite it. The first distance assigned is the shortest, and we never touch it again.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"C","visitedIds":["A","B","C"],"frontierIds":["D"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2"},"queue":[{"label":"D","sub":"d=2"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["D"],"edgeHighlights":[["C","D"]]},"vars":[["from","C"],["neighbour","D"],["known?","yes (d=2)"]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at C's neighbour E. E has no distance yet → it's brand new. We reach it from C, so dist[E] = dist[C] + 1 = 2. This is necessarily a shortest path: C sat on the smallest possible ring, and E is exactly one edge further out.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"C","visitedIds":["A","B","C"],"frontierIds":["D"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2"},"queue":[{"label":"D","sub":"d=2"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["E"],"edgeHighlights":[["C","E"]]},"vars":[["from","C"],["neighbour","E"],["known?","no → set"]]},{"codeLine":8,"narration":"Set dist[E] = 2 and ENQUEUE E at the BACK (it turns blue, discovered, awaiting its turn). Because the queue is FIFO, every node already waiting (all on ring 1 or earlier) gets visited before E. That ordering is exactly what keeps BFS expanding ring by ring.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"C","visitedIds":["A","B","C"],"frontierIds":["D","E"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2"},"queue":[{"label":"D","sub":"d=2"},{"label":"E","sub":"d=2"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["E"],"edgeHighlights":[["C","E"]]},"vars":[["set","dist[E]=2"],["enqueue","E"],["queue size",2]]},{"codeLine":5,"narration":"DEQUEUE the front: node D. We pop it from the front of the queue and VISIT it (paint it green). Its distance dist[D] = 2 is now final, it can never be improved, because BFS reached it on the earliest possible ring.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"D","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D"],"frontierIds":["E"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2"},"queue":[{"label":"E","sub":"d=2"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A"},"vars":[["dequeued","D"],["dist","D=2"],["queue size",1]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at D's neighbour B. B already has a distance (dist[B] = 1), it was discovered on an equal or earlier ring, so we DON'T overwrite it. The first distance assigned is the shortest, and we never touch it again.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"D","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D"],"frontierIds":["E"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2"},"queue":[{"label":"E","sub":"d=2"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["B"],"edgeHighlights":[["D","B"]]},"vars":[["from","D"],["neighbour","B"],["known?","yes (d=1)"]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at D's neighbour C. C already has a distance (dist[C] = 1), it was discovered on an equal or earlier ring, so we DON'T overwrite it. The first distance assigned is the shortest, and we never touch it again.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"D","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D"],"frontierIds":["E"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2"},"queue":[{"label":"E","sub":"d=2"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["C"],"edgeHighlights":[["D","C"]]},"vars":[["from","D"],["neighbour","C"],["known?","yes (d=1)"]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at D's neighbour F. F has no distance yet → it's brand new. We reach it from D, so dist[F] = dist[D] + 1 = 3. This is necessarily a shortest path: D sat on the smallest possible ring, and F is exactly one edge further out.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"D","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D"],"frontierIds":["E"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2"},"queue":[{"label":"E","sub":"d=2"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["F"],"edgeHighlights":[["D","F"]]},"vars":[["from","D"],["neighbour","F"],["known?","no → set"]]},{"codeLine":8,"narration":"Set dist[F] = 3 and ENQUEUE F at the BACK (it turns blue, discovered, awaiting its turn). Because the queue is FIFO, every node already waiting (all on ring 2 or earlier) gets visited before F. That ordering is exactly what keeps BFS expanding ring by ring.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"D","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D"],"frontierIds":["E","F"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2","F":"d=3"},"queue":[{"label":"E","sub":"d=2"},{"label":"F","sub":"d=3"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["F"],"edgeHighlights":[["D","F"]]},"vars":[["set","dist[F]=3"],["enqueue","F"],["queue size",2]]},{"codeLine":5,"narration":"DEQUEUE the front: node E. We pop it from the front of the queue and VISIT it (paint it green). Its distance dist[E] = 2 is now final, it can never be improved, because BFS reached it on the earliest possible ring.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"E","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D","E"],"frontierIds":["F"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2","F":"d=3"},"queue":[{"label":"F","sub":"d=3"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A"},"vars":[["dequeued","E"],["dist","E=2"],["queue size",1]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at E's neighbour C. C already has a distance (dist[C] = 1), it was discovered on an equal or earlier ring, so we DON'T overwrite it. The first distance assigned is the shortest, and we never touch it again.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"E","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D","E"],"frontierIds":["F"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2","F":"d=3"},"queue":[{"label":"F","sub":"d=3"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["C"],"edgeHighlights":[["E","C"]]},"vars":[["from","E"],["neighbour","C"],["known?","yes (d=1)"]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at E's neighbour F. F already has a distance (dist[F] = 3), it was discovered on an equal or earlier ring, so we DON'T overwrite it. The first distance assigned is the shortest, and we never touch it again.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"E","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D","E"],"frontierIds":["F"],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2","F":"d=3"},"queue":[{"label":"F","sub":"d=3"}],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["F"],"edgeHighlights":[["E","F"]]},"vars":[["from","E"],["neighbour","F"],["known?","yes (d=3)"]]},{"codeLine":5,"narration":"DEQUEUE the front: node F. We pop it from the front of the queue and VISIT it (paint it green). Its distance dist[F] = 3 is now final, it can never be improved, because BFS reached it on the earliest possible ring.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"F","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D","E","F"],"frontierIds":[],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2","F":"d=3"},"queue":[],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A"},"vars":[["dequeued","F"],["dist","F=3"],["queue size",0]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at F's neighbour D. D already has a distance (dist[D] = 2), it was discovered on an equal or earlier ring, so we DON'T overwrite it. The first distance assigned is the shortest, and we never touch it again.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"F","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D","E","F"],"frontierIds":[],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2","F":"d=3"},"queue":[],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["D"],"edgeHighlights":[["F","D"]]},"vars":[["from","F"],["neighbour","D"],["known?","yes (d=2)"]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"Look at F's neighbour E. E already has a distance (dist[E] = 2), it was discovered on an equal or earlier ring, so we DON'T overwrite it. The first distance assigned is the shortest, and we never touch it again.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"F","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D","E","F"],"frontierIds":[],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2","F":"d=3"},"queue":[],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["E"],"edgeHighlights":[["F","E"]]},"vars":[["from","F"],["neighbour","E"],["known?","yes (d=2)"]]},{"codeLine":10,"narration":"Queue empty → BFS is done. Every node now carries its shortest-path distance from A: B=1, C=1, D=2, E=2, F=3. Look at F: it sits on ring 3, reached the very first time via A→B→D→F (or A→C→E→F), both exactly 3 edges. BFS guaranteed that the FIRST arrival was shortest, so we never had to reconsider F.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"$undefined","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D","E","F"],"frontierIds":[],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2","F":"d=3"},"queue":[],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["F"]},"vars":[["dist[F]",3],["shortest","A→B→D→F (3)"]]},{"codeLine":10,"narration":"Why does DFS NOT give this? DFS plunges down ONE path as far as it can before backing up. It might reach F via the long way first and record that, having no notion of rings, so a DFS distance is just \"some path\", not the shortest. BFS's level-by-level expansion is the whole reason its first-touch distance is minimal.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"$undefined","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D","E","F"],"frontierIds":[],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2","F":"d=3"},"queue":[],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A","highlightIds":["F"]},"vars":[["BFS","shortest path"],["DFS","some path"]]},{"codeLine":10,"narration":"Cost: every node is enqueued and dequeued exactly once (the distance check stops re-discovery), and every edge is examined a constant number of times. That gives O(V + E) time and O(V) space for the queue, the visited set, and the distance map. Linear in the size of the graph, and you get shortest paths for free.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","activeId":"$undefined","visitedIds":["A","B","C","D","E","F"],"frontierIds":[],"notes":{"A":"d=0","B":"d=1","C":"d=1","D":"d=2","E":"d=2","F":"d=3"},"queue":[],"queueLabel":"queue (FIFO)","label":"undirected graph: BFS from A"},"vars":[["time","O(V+E)"],["space","O(V)"]]}]}]},"patternTitle":"Breadth-First Search"}]
